1e:["$","div",null,{"className":"relative","children":["$","$L1f",null,{"asset":{"id":"HA-102","title":"Improved Washing-Machine","inventor":"Caroline F. Fleming","patent":"Pat. #231,554 • 1880","patentDate":"August 24, 1880","location":"Texas Homestead","category":"DOMESTIC","categoryLabel":"Domestic Mechanics","badge":"Kinetic Model","image":"/images/character_fleming.jpg","desc":"Reciprocating agitator mechanism reducing manual laundering wear through fluid currents.","polyCount":"76,000 Quads","medium":"Cedar Wood • Cast Iron Crank","resonance":"Manual 60 RPM Agitation","era":"Golden Age (1871-1910)","modelType":"fleming","schematicImage":"/images/character_fleming.jpg","videoDuration":"0:30","story":"In late 19th-century America, laundry was among the most grueling, caustic, and hazardous household chores, requiring hours of boiling lye water and abrasive scrubbing over corrugated washboards. Caroline F. Fleming of Texas patented an ingeniously balanced mechanical agitator that dramatically reduced physical exertion and wear on garments, representing an essential triumph in female-led engineering.","mechanics":"Fleming's machine utilizes a sealed wooden drum fitted with an interior radial paddle agitator driven by a geared hand-crank. Instead of merely spinning the water, the mechanism creates opposing turbulent currents that force soapy water through textile fibers without tearing delicate seams.","legacy":"Fleming's patent laid the direct mechanical groundwork for modern horizontal-axis and top-loading washing machines. Her innovation freed millions of households from hours of exhausting manual labor, creating a social and economic shift towards domestic automation."},"isStandalonePage":true}]}]
